ligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 54\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 30\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 & -10 \\\\ -18 & 54 & 30\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{3}+3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{3}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n12a+\\frac{b}{c}=12\\cdot (-\\frac{5}{3})+\\frac{3}{1}=-17.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415144 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186415145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415144 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "40c07ba2-e36f-5f6c-9e19-b8d5be6314d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415144 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186415139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415145 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186415148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415145 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b7f52acc-285e-5a90-8147-1ce492061ed5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415145 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186415141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415146 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186415144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415146 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c28e7ab5-a0fd-5181-b924-09cdf36224af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415146 #edb/ref 1063